# VENDOR_ALLOWLIST - this project's own waivers for the vendor-collateral scanner
#
# READ BY  ASIC/asic-toolkit/ci/check-vendor-collateral.sh, which finds this file
#          at the top of the repository it is scanning. The toolkit ships NO
#          project's waivers; every entry below belongs to THIS repository and
#          travels with it. A second project adopting the toolkit starts with an
#          empty table, which is the only honest starting point - inheriting
#          another chip's exemptions on day one is a hole the size of that list.
#
# FORMAT   <path glob> | <rules waived, comma separated> | <reason> | <owner>
#
#          Four fields, none empty, and a reason a reviewer can disagree with.
#          The scanner validates this file with the same rules it applies to its
#          own shipped table and ABORTS on a malformed one, because a table it
#          cannot parse is a table whose waiver decisions cannot be trusted.
#          Blank lines and lines beginning with # are ignored.
#
# SCOPE    The waiver is PER RULE and per path. A file exempted from one rule is
#          still scanned by every other. `file.sha` cannot be waived here at all:
#          a known vendor file is a known vendor file wherever it is moved to.
#
# A GLOB HERE SPANS `/`. The scanner matches with shell `case`, whose `*` crosses
# a directory separator, so anything ending in `/*` waives an entire SUBTREE.
# Write the subtree you mean; the scanner refuses a glob of `*` outright.
#
# EVERY ENTRY IS PRINTED ON EVERY RUN, whatever the verdict. An allowlist nobody
# sees is an allowlist nobody prunes, and the scanner reports as
# `vendor.allowlist.stale` any entry here whose rule no longer fires on anything
# the glob matches. When it says that about a line below, delete the line.
#
# Copyright (C) 2026, SoC Labs (www.soclabs.org)
#-----------------------------------------------------------------------------

